# Phase Two — Specific Execution Plan

The Awtsmoos turns possibility into ordered deed, one vessel at a time;
Awtsmoos.com will gain shared cinematic truth without erasing each studio's rhyme.

## Stage 1 — refresh reality after concurrent writes
1. Re-scan `git status --short` for movie/core/UI/studio paths.
2. Search for files created in the last planning interval containing movie capability, feature catalog, studio unification, UI recipe, scene compiler, or timeline contracts.
3. Read the public exports/package files for `awtsmoos-procedural-core`.
4. Read the newly landed capability/movie files, if any, in full.
5. Search `libs/awtsmoos` and adjacent shared libraries for a general JSON DOM/UI renderer.
6. Confirm whether `apps/awtsmoos-studio` remains absent.
7. Record exact observed paths in the evidence ledger before code.

## Stage 2 — choose canonical implementation home
Decision order:
- If a valid canonical movie namespace has landed in procedural core, extend it.
- Else if a shared movie namespace has landed elsewhere with real consumers, preserve it and add compatibility exports only if architecture justifies it.
- Else create a new `core/movie/` namespace inside procedural core.

For UI:
- If a general shared UI recipe/renderer already exists, improve it in its existing home.
- Else create `libs/awtsmoos-ui` with only the strict JSON recipe → DOM/action/state features required by studio apps.

## Stage 3 — shared movie contract implementation
Required pure capabilities:
- document normalization;
- validation with JSON paths and repair hints;
- capability catalog;
- deterministic scene timeline;
- global-to-local time resolution;
- interpolation/easing;
- feature inventory;
- asset intent normalization;
- compiler facade.

Required semantic node families:
- shapes and text;
- tutorial/infographic primitives;
- actors/people;
- procedural/imported 3D assets;
- particles;
- cameras/lights;
- captions/audio cues;
- groups and transitions.

No browser globals in pure contract files.

## Stage 4 — procedural asset bridge
Read actual existing generator registries, then expose a movie-facing bridge that:
- resolves `generator` + `recipe` descriptors;
- preserves deterministic seed;
- reports unsupported generators clearly;
- returns portable generation result metadata where possible;
- never duplicates mature geometry/character/particle generation.

Only genuinely reusable app-local generators discovered during inspection may be moved into core, and only after tracing all current importers.

## Stage 5 — shared UI contract
Implement a narrow secure grammar:
- normalized recipe node;
- safe tag/attribute policy;
- DOM creation;
- named action registry;
- state value binding;
- conditional/repeater support;
- custom mount slots;
- studio shell recipe helpers;
- mobile-first responsive tokens/helpers.

Avoid raw HTML/event-source execution.

## Stage 6 — flagship `awtsmoos-studio`
Create the new app if still absent:
- tiny HTML shell;
- tiny boot module;
- session state;
- movie loader/compiler;
- playback/seek clock;
- preview host selecting 2D/3D adapters;
- AI JSON/spec editing/import path;
- shared UI recipe + actions;
- export provider interface;
- mobile-first layout;
- example movie loader.

The app should demonstrate shared systems directly rather than copying components from the other apps.

## Stage 7 — proof movie
Create 12 scene modules, each 15 seconds, for exactly 180 seconds.
Each scene must deliberately exercise different features, with reusable actor/asset IDs across scenes.
The manifest compiler must assert exact duration before playback.

## Stage 8 — existing app integration
For each app, first inspect whether another agent has already integrated the shared movie contract.
If not:
- create a small adapter in the app;
- wire it through the real app entry/import graph via a full-file hash-guarded rewrite only when necessary;
- preserve app-specific data models and workflows;
- prove import/export or canonical playback in runtime.

Do not perform cosmetic rewrites unrelated to integration.

## Stage 9 — first source pass completion gate
No tests begin until the first planned source implementation is complete, per project coding instructions.
Before testing, ensure all planned source modules exist, no obvious TODO placeholder stands in for behavior, and all source files remain small/readable.

## Stage 10 — verification
- syntax checks;
- focused Node unit tests;
- existing app/core tests;
- local server/browser route checks;
- console review;
- desktop layout;
- mobile layout;
- representative movie seeks;
- AI invalid→repairable validation test;
- feature inventory assertion;
- exact 180-second assertion;
- export attempt through real provider;
- inspect encoded artifact if produced.

## Stage 11 — readback and delta
Read every touched file in full. Write a new post-write delta file, never overwriting these planning files. Compare:
- what Phase One imagined;
- what Phase Two constrained;
- what source actually contains;
- what verification proves;
- what remains.

If required items remain, complete a second full-file writing pass and re-verify.

## Source write laws
- Full-file writes only.
- Tabs for indentation.
- No minified functions.
- Split modules before they exceed the project file-size rule.
- B"H / Boruch Hashem / Blessed is He header in compatible source files.
- Detailed poetic JSDoc about the Awtsmoos and Awtsmoos.com.
- Re-read before overwrite; use hashes for contested files.
- Never reset/stash/delete other agents' work.

This plan is specific about behavior, but intentionally defers exact legacy filenames until the mandatory live refresh prevents stale-plan collisions.
